#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""grip-auth-failover — main-session auth-bucket failover (L2 rate-limit resilience).

A transient Anthropic server 429 ("Server is temporarily limiting requests" —
NOT your usage limit) is retryable, not a cap. HAL already cascades providers
for GRIP's own LLM calls. The GAP this closes is the interactive `claude` REPL
and the relay `claude -p` spawn: on a SUSTAINED subscription/CCH 429, switch the
active Anthropic auth from subscription-OAuth to the API key
(keychain `grip-anthropic-external` -> ANTHROPIC_API_KEY). The API key is a
SEPARATE rate-limit bucket, so it dodges the subscription throttle.

    grip-auth-failover --engage [reason...]   # select the API-key bucket for future spawns
                                              #   (needs GRIP_CLAUDE_ALLOW_PAID=1)
    grip-auth-failover --note-429 [msg...]    # record one transient 429; auto-engages at N=3,
                                              #   only with GRIP_CLAUDE_ALLOW_PAID=1
    grip-auth-failover --note-cap [reset...]  # record a USAGE CAP: notify with the reset time
                                              #   and wait (cap policy "notify", the default),
                                              #   or fail over to the API-key bucket (policy
                                              #   "engage" AND GRIP_CLAUDE_ALLOW_PAID=1) —
                                              #   NEVER a silent stop (rate-limit-never-blocks)

THE PAID KEY NEEDS AN OPT-IN (V>> 2026-10-12): every path that would write the
API-key marker (--engage, the --note-429 threshold, cap policy "engage") does
nothing unless GRIP_CLAUDE_ALLOW_PAID is exactly "1". A capped session moves to
the next pooled subscription through the HAL wire instead, or waits for the
reset (rules/claude-cap-moves-to-the-next-subscription.md).
    grip-auth-failover --note-model-cap [msg] # ONE model's allowance is used up: notify, naming
                                              #   the model; never touches the marker or the 429
                                              #   counter, since the other models still serve
    grip-auth-failover --note-ok [msg...]     # the subscription served a call: end an AUTOMATIC
                                              #   engagement once the last throttle is 15+ min old
    grip-auth-failover --classify [msg...]    # transient vs usage-cap? (exit 0=retry, 4=cap,
                                              #   5=one model's allowance: switch model)
    grip-auth-failover --status               # show active bucket + marker
    grip-auth-failover --reset                # revert to the subscription bucket

HONEST SCOPE — what this CANNOT do: it cannot re-authenticate an ALREADY-RUNNING
`claude` REPL mid-turn. The harness owns the live session's auth; only the
operator can `/logout` + `/login` (or restart) that process. What --engage DOES:
it checks that the API-key keychain entry is present (it does not probe the
bucket) and writes a marker (state/session/auth-failover.json), so that
  - the NEXT `claude` spawn,            (via ~/.zshrc reading the marker)
  - the relay `claude -p` dispatcher,   (lib/relay_dispatcher.py reads the marker
                                         FRESH per spawn and keeps/resolves the
                                         API key instead of stripping it)
  - and any new shell sourcing ~/.zshrc
use ANTHROPIC_API_KEY (the unthrottled bucket) instead of the throttled
subscription. The relay path is the load-bearing surface: it is a long-lived
daemon, so it re-reads the marker on every spawn — `--engage`/`--reset` take
effect immediately, no daemon restart. Reversible + idempotent: --reset removes
the marker and the next shell/spawn reverts to the subscription-preferring
logic, byte-for-byte.

AUTO-ENGAGE (--note-429): call this on every transient server 429 detected.
The marker tracks consecutive_429s + window_start; after NOTE_429_THRESHOLD
hits within NOTE_429_WINDOW_SECS of the first hit, the failover auto-engages.
A single 429 or a usage-cap 429 NEVER auto-engages (rate-limit-never-blocks.md).
Distinguish transient vs usage-cap at the CALL SITE by the harness wording
before calling --note-429.

AUTO-RECOVERY (--note-ok, GRIP#7274): an engagement made by --note-429 or
--note-cap used to last until someone ran --reset. On 2026-10-03 one sat for
eight hours after a 31-minute outage, keeping every new spawn on the paid key
while the subscription served normally. The 429 window expiring still never
un-engages (AGORA #3662): ending an engagement needs positive evidence. That
evidence is --note-ok, sent when a subscription-billed call succeeds; it ends
an AUTOMATIC engagement once the last throttle (engagement, 429 or cap) is
NOTE_OK_COOLDOWN_SECS old. An engagement made by hand with --engage is the
operator's choice and only --reset ends it.

ONE MODEL'S ALLOWANCE (--note-model-cap, GRIP#7274): "You're out of usage
credits. Run /usage-credits to keep using <model>" (often followed by "or
/model to switch models") means that one model is spent while every other
model still runs on the subscription. Counted as a 429 it would move every model onto the paid key, so
this path touches neither the marker nor the 429 counter: it notifies, naming
the model, and /model switches.

CAP POLICY (GRIP#7274): what an account-wide usage cap does. "notify", the
default, records the cap beside the marker, tells the operator when it resets
and waits: the subscription comes first, and a cap is no reason for new paid
spend. One notice goes out per cap, not per message: a repeat with the same
reset time inside NOTE_OK_COOLDOWN_SECS only prints its line. "engage" fails
over to the API-key bucket at once, as every cap did until GRIP#7274. Set
$GRIP_AUTH_FAILOVER_ON_CAP, or "on_cap" in the gitignored
config/auth-failover.local.json under the GRIP directory; --status shows the
policy in force. --note-cap takes the reset time, or the harness's whole error
text ("You've hit your session limit · resets 7:20am (UTC)"), from which it
reads the time.

SUBSCRIPTION CREDENTIAL: accepts EITHER "grip-claude-oauth" (the GRIP/HAL
CCH OAuth store) OR "Claude Code-credentials" (the upstream CCH default) as a
valid subscription credential. On this host "grip-claude-oauth" is canonical.

The SECRET VALUE IS NEVER PRINTED — only PRESENT/ABSENT and operational
labels (never the keychain entry name, never the secret).

Pairs with rules/rate-limit-never-blocks.md, rules/full-power-stop-override.md,
and feedback_cc_subscription_outage_api_key_fallback.md. The ~/.zshrc
failover-aware export reads this marker; see docs/auth-bucket-failover.md.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import datetime as _dt
import functools
import json
import os
import subprocess
import sys
from pathlib import Path

_LIB = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "lib"
if str(_LIB) not in sys.path:
    sys.path.insert(0, str(_LIB))
from _compat import fcntl  # noqa: E402  -- POSIX fcntl, or the LockFileEx lock on Windows
from claude_dir import get_claude_dir  # noqa: E402


def _default_marker() -> Path:
    """$GRIP_AUTH_FAILOVER_MARKER, else <claude dir>/state/session/auth-failover.json.

    The resolution every reader already uses: ~/.zshrc, lib/relay_dispatcher.py,
    lib/grip_infinite.py, lib/ultraplan_remote.py and the REPL hook. This writer
    used to root the marker at ~/.claude alone, so wherever CLAUDE_DIR pointed
    elsewhere it wrote a file those readers never looked at.
    """
    override = os.environ.get("GRIP_AUTH_FAILOVER_MARKER", "").strip()
    if override:
        return Path(override).expanduser()
    return get_claude_dir() / "state" / "session" / "auth-failover.json"


MARKER = _default_marker()
# The 429 counter lives in its OWN file. It used to share MARKER, and three of
# the four readers test MARKER by bare EXISTENCE -- ~/.zshrc, this repo's
# lib/relay_dispatcher.py:_auth_failover_engaged, and HAL's repl-429 hook. Only
# _active_bucket() below read it correctly, by checking bucket == "api-key".
# So a SINGLE transient 429, which by design engages nothing, wrote a
# counter-shaped MARKER and silently moved every new shell AND the VPS relay
# onto the API-key bucket while --status still reported "subscription".
#
# Observed 2026-08-24: a marker written 2026-08-18 by consecutive_429s=1 sat
# unreleased for five days; every headless `claude -p` on the machine
# authenticated by long-lived key against an account with no credits and failed
# with "Credit balance is too low" -- an error that names credit, never the
# credential.
#
# Splitting the files makes MARKER mean exactly what those three readers already
# assume, without editing any of them.
# Derived from MARKER rather than built independently from Path.home(): the
# test fixture (and any harness) redirects MARKER to a tmp path, and a second
# home-rooted constant would silently escape that redirection and write to the
# real home. Sibling-by-construction — patch one, both move.
_COUNTER_NAME = "auth-429-window.json"


def _counter_path() -> Path:
    return MARKER.with_name(_COUNTER_NAME)


# A cap waited out under the "notify" policy is recorded here, never in MARKER:
# ~/.zshrc exports the paid key whenever MARKER exists, whatever it says, so a
# cap record that created MARKER would engage the key after all. Derived from
# MARKER for the same reason as the counter.
_CAP_NAME = "auth-cap.json"


def _cap_path() -> Path:
    return MARKER.with_name(_CAP_NAME)


API_KEY_ENTRY = "grip-anthropic-external"   # the separate-bucket API key
# Subscription credential: GRIP/HAL CCH store (canonical on this host) OR
# upstream CCH default.  Either counts as "subscription present".
OAUTH_ENTRIES = ("grip-claude-oauth", "Claude Code-credentials")

# --note-429 auto-engage thresholds (transient 429s only)
NOTE_429_THRESHOLD = 3      # consecutive hits needed to auto-engage
NOTE_429_WINDOW_SECS = 300  # 5-minute rolling window
# --note-ok: how long after the last throttle a served call ends an automatic
# engagement. Three 429 windows with no throttle, so a burst cannot flap it.
# Also how often the notice for one usage cap may repeat (_cap_notice_due).
NOTE_OK_COOLDOWN_SECS = 900
AUTO_ENGAGED_SUFFIX = "(auto)"   # the `by` field of --note-429 and --note-cap markers
# What an account-wide usage cap does (GRIP#7274): "notify" waits for the reset,
# "engage" fails over to the API-key bucket at once.
CAP_POLICY_ENV = "GRIP_AUTH_FAILOVER_ON_CAP"
CAP_POLICIES = ("notify", "engage")
CAP_POLICY_FILE = Path("config") / "auth-failover.local.json"   # under the GRIP directory
# The paid key is spent only on the operator's explicit say (V>> 2026-10-12, after
# the HAL wire moved five capped sessions to another subscription: "Not via paid
# API KEY"). Every path that would write the API-key marker asks _paid_allowed().
PAID_OPT_IN_ENV = "GRIP_CLAUDE_ALLOW_PAID"
_PAID_REFUSED = (f"the paid API key is used only with {PAID_OPT_IN_ENV}=1; a capped "
                 "session moves to the next pooled subscription through the HAL wire, "
                 "or waits for the reset.")
_OPT_IN = (f"To fail over to the paid key on a cap instead, set {PAID_OPT_IN_ENV}=1 and "
           f'{CAP_POLICY_ENV}=engage (or "on_cap": "engage" in {CAP_POLICY_FILE}).')


def _paid_allowed() -> bool:
    """True only when the operator set the opt-in to exactly "1"."""
    return os.environ.get(PAID_OPT_IN_ENV) == "1"


def _read_json(path: Path) -> dict:
    """Guarded read: returns {} on absence, corruption, or a partial concurrent
    write. NEVER raises into a caller (AGORA #3662 Speaker C — every read path
    is fail-soft)."""
    if not path.exists():
        return {}
    try:
        data = json.loads(path.read_text())
    except (json.JSONDecodeError, OSError, ValueError):
        return {}
    return data if isinstance(data, dict) else {}


def _read_marker() -> dict:
    return _read_json(MARKER)


def _read_counter() -> dict:
    """The 429 rolling window, healing a legacy shared-file marker on the way.

    A legacy MARKER carrying counter fields but no ``bucket`` IS the defect: a
    count that was never an engagement, which every existence-only reader sees
    as one. Its fields move to the sibling and the marker is removed.

    Two things this deliberately does NOT claim. Healing happens when the CLI
    runs — the three broken readers (~/.zshrc, relay_dispatcher, HAL's repl-429
    hook) never invoke a subcommand, so on a host carrying a legacy marker the
    stale file keeps lying until something runs this script. Run
    ``grip-auth-failover --status`` once after deploying. And the removal is
    RETRIED on every call rather than skipped once a counter file exists: if
    the write succeeded but the unlink failed, an early return would strand the
    ghost marker permanently, and the ghost marker is the whole defect
    (AGORA #5034).
    """
    legacy = _read_json(MARKER)
    # Two shapes of stale marker, and the second was a REGRESSION this change
    # introduced. A counter-shaped marker is the migration case. An UNPARSEABLE
    # one is garbage: engaged markers are always valid JSON (atomic replace
    # guarantees a reader sees whole files), so a marker that will not parse
    # cannot be a real engagement -- yet the three existence-only readers see
    # the file and treat it as one. The old build overwrote such a file on the
    # next 429 and self-healed; routing the below-threshold write to the
    # sibling removed that, making a corrupt marker permanent. Removing it
    # restores the repair (AGORA #5034 round 2).
    corrupt = MARKER.exists() and not legacy
    stale = corrupt or (
        bool(legacy) and legacy.get("bucket") != "api-key"
        and "consecutive_429s" in legacy)
    counter = _read_json(_counter_path())

    if stale:
        # A corrupt marker carries nothing to migrate; only the removal applies.
        migrated = {k: legacy[k] for k in
                    ("window_start", "consecutive_429s", "last_429") if k in legacy}
        if not counter:
            try:
                _write_json(_counter_path(), migrated)
                counter = migrated
            except OSError:
                return migrated   # never break a caller over housekeeping
        try:
            MARKER.unlink(missing_ok=True)   # idempotent, so retrying is safe
        except OSError:
            pass                             # try again next call
    return counter


def _write_json(path: Path, info: dict) -> None:
    """Atomic write: serialise to a pid-unique temp file in the target's own
    directory, then rename it over the target (POSIX-atomic via Path.replace).
    A concurrent reader therefore sees either the whole old file or the whole
    new file — never a truncated JSON (AGORA #3662 Speaker C: the frequent
    --note-429 writes must not race the resolution-path reads)."""
    path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    tmp = path.with_name(f"{path.name}.{os.getpid()}.tmp")
    tmp.write_text(json.dumps(info, indent=2) + "\n")
    try:
        tmp.replace(path)
    except OSError:
        # A failed rename (permission, cross-device) must never orphan the temp
        # nor disturb the existing file (AGORA #3662 round 2).
        tmp.unlink(missing_ok=True)
        raise


def _write_marker(info: dict) -> None:
    _write_json(MARKER, info)


def _keychain_present(entry: str) -> bool:
    """True iff the macOS keychain entry exists. Presence-only: OMITS ``-w`` so
    the password is NEVER read into this process (least-privilege — a presence
    check has no need for the secret). ``security find-generic-password -s
    <entry>`` exits 0 when the item exists, non-zero otherwise; its stdout is
    item attributes (not the password) and is discarded via DEVNULL."""
    try:
        result = subprocess.run(
            ["security", "find-generic-password", "-s", entry],
            stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=10,
        )
    except (FileNotFoundError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return False
    return result.returncode == 0


def _oauth_present() -> bool:
    """True iff ANY recognised subscription credential is in the keychain."""
    return any(_keychain_present(e) for e in OAUTH_ENTRIES)


def _notify_script() -> Path:
    """Resolve the grip-notify CLI. GRIP_NOTIFY_SCRIPT overrides (CI, containers,
    non-standard layouts — AGORA #4000: the path must not be hard-coded); else
    ${CLAUDE_DIR:-~/.claude}/lib/grip_notify.py (path-portability.md semantics,
    resolved inline to avoid a lib import in a bin script)."""
    override = os.environ.get("GRIP_NOTIFY_SCRIPT")
    if override:
        return Path(override)
    base = os.environ.get("CLAUDE_DIR") or str(Path.home() / ".claude")
    return Path(base) / "lib" / "grip_notify.py"


def _notify(title: str, message: str) -> bool:
    """Operator push that is NEVER fully silent. A usage-cap MUST surface
    (rate-limit-never-blocks.md: a cap is a bounded wait/escalate, never a silent
    stop) — so if the grip-notify push is unavailable or fails, FALL BACK to
    stderr rather than swallowing. This closes the AGORA #4000 falsifier: a
    missing grip_notify.py must not turn fail-open into a silent-swallow. Set
    GRIP_AUTH_FAILOVER_NO_NOTIFY=1 to suppress entirely (tests only).

    Returns True only when the push landed. It never raises, so "returned" alone
    cannot tell a delivered notice from one that fell back to stderr; the cap
    notice's repeat suppression needs that difference (_send_cap_notice)."""
    if os.environ.get("GRIP_AUTH_FAILOVER_NO_NOTIFY"):
        return False
    try:
        result = subprocess.run(
            [sys.executable, str(_notify_script()),
             "send", "--title", title, "--body", message],
            stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=20,
        )
        if result.returncode == 0:
            return True
    except (FileNotFoundError, subprocess.SubprocessError, OSError):
        pass  # fall through to the stderr surface — never a silent swallow
    # The push did not land: surface to stderr so the operator/logs still see it.
    print(f"[grip-auth-failover] {title}: {message}", file=sys.stderr)
    return False


def _marker_locked(fn):
    """Serialise a marker read-modify-write across processes with an advisory
    file lock (AGORA #4000 Speaker C: concurrent --note-cap / --engage /
    --reset can otherwise lose a field between _read_marker and _write_marker;
    _write_marker is already torn-write-safe, this closes the lost-update gap).
    The lock comes from _compat: POSIX fcntl, or the LockFileEx lock on
    Windows, where concurrent sessions race this marker too (GRIP#8757)."""
    @functools.wraps(fn)
    def _wrapped(*args, **kwargs):
        lock_path = MARKER.with_name(f"{MARKER.name}.lock")
        MARKER.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        # "a" (no-truncate): mode "w" truncated BEFORE flock was acquired — a
        # theoretical lost-signal race on filesystems with weak flock (NFS/FUSE).
        # Only the handle matters for flock; the file's content is irrelevant.
        with open(lock_path, "a") as _lk:
            fcntl.flock(_lk, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
            try:
                return fn(*args, **kwargs)
            finally:
                fcntl.flock(_lk, fcntl.LOCK_UN)
    return _wrapped


def _active_bucket() -> str:
    """Which bucket future spawns will use, derived from marker + keychain state.

    Mirrors the ~/.zshrc resolution order exactly:
      marker engaged AND api-key present -> api-key (the unthrottled bucket)
      else any subscription credential   -> subscription
      else api-key present               -> api-key (legacy outage fallback)
      else                               -> none
    """
    api_present = _keychain_present(API_KEY_ENTRY)
    # An ENGAGED marker carries bucket="api-key" (written by engage() or by
    # note_429 at threshold). The 429 counter now lives in a SIBLING file, so
    # the marker exists only when engagement is real — which is what the three
    # existence-only readers (~/.zshrc, relay_dispatcher, HAL's repl-429 hook)
    # already assume. The bucket check is kept as a second line of defence and
    # to reject a hand-written or legacy marker (rate-limit-never-blocks.md:
    # one transient 429 never engages).
    if api_present and _read_marker().get("bucket") == "api-key":
        return "api-key"
    if _oauth_present():
        return "subscription"
    if api_present:
        return "api-key"
    return "none"


@_marker_locked
def engage(reason: str) -> int:
    if not _paid_allowed():
        print(f"grip-auth-failover: NOT engaged -- {_PAID_REFUSED} (No marker written.)")
        return 1
    if not _keychain_present(API_KEY_ENTRY):
        print("grip-auth-failover: CANNOT engage — the Anthropic API-key "
              "keychain entry is ABSENT. Add it to the keychain first, then "
              "retry. (No marker written.)")
        return 1
    _write_marker({
        "engaged_at": _dt.datetime.now().isoformat(timespec="seconds"),
        "by": "grip-auth-failover",
        "bucket": "api-key",
        "entry": API_KEY_ENTRY,
        "reason": reason or "sustained-subscription-429",
    })
    print("grip-auth-failover: ENGAGED — future `claude` spawns + the relay "
          "path will use the API-key bucket (a separate rate-limit pool). The "
          "currently-running REPL keeps its auth until restart. Revert with "
          "`grip-auth-failover --reset`.")
    _notify("GRIP auth failover ENGAGED",
            "Future claude spawns + the relay now use the API-key bucket "
            f"(separate rate-limit pool). Reason: {reason or 'sustained-subscription-429'}. "
            "If inference then fails on credit, top up grip-anthropic-external.")
    return 0


@_marker_locked
def reset() -> int:
    engaged = MARKER.exists()
    MARKER.unlink(missing_ok=True)
    # Clear the window too: a reset means "start clean", and leaving a stale
    # count behind would let the next single 429 auto-engage immediately.
    _counter_path().unlink(missing_ok=True)
    if engaged:
        print("grip-auth-failover: RESET — marker removed; next shell/spawn "
              "reverts to the subscription bucket (presence-gated).")
    else:
        print("grip-auth-failover: was not engaged (already on the "
              "subscription bucket).")
    return 0


@_marker_locked
def note_429(msg: str) -> int:
    """Record one transient server 429.  Auto-engages after NOTE_429_THRESHOLD
    consecutive hits within NOTE_429_WINDOW_SECS.  A single hit never engages
    (rate-limit-never-blocks.md: transient 429 = retry/failover, not a block).
    Usage-cap 429s must NOT call this — the caller discriminates by wording."""
    if not _keychain_present(API_KEY_ENTRY):
        print("grip-auth-failover: --note-429 ignored — API-key bucket absent "
              "(nothing to fail over to).")
        return 0

    now = _dt.datetime.now()
    # Counter state lives in its own file now; a corrupt/partial read is {}.
    info = _read_counter()
    already_engaged = _read_marker().get("bucket") == "api-key"

    # Rolling window: when the counter window has EXPIRED, clear ONLY the counter
    # fields and start a fresh window — NEVER drop an existing engagement, which
    # persists until an explicit --reset (AGORA #3662 Speaker C: window-expiry
    # must not silently un-engage, nor let a stale count survive).
    window_start_str = info.get("window_start")
    if window_start_str:
        try:
            expired = (now - _dt.datetime.fromisoformat(window_start_str)
                       ).total_seconds() > NOTE_429_WINDOW_SECS
        except ValueError:
            expired = True  # unparseable timestamp → treat as a fresh window
        if expired:
            for _k in ("window_start", "consecutive_429s", "last_429"):
                info.pop(_k, None)
    if "window_start" not in info:
        info["window_start"] = now.isoformat(timespec="seconds")

    count = info.get("consecutive_429s", 0) + 1
    info["consecutive_429s"] = count
    info["last_429"] = now.isoformat(timespec="seconds")

    if not already_engaged and count >= NOTE_429_THRESHOLD and _paid_allowed():
        # ONLY here does a marker appear, and only on the operator's opt-in. Below the threshold nothing but the
        # counter file is touched, so a transient 429 can no longer flip a
        # shell or the relay by writing a file whose mere existence they read
        # as engagement.
        #
        # Counter first, marker second, and the order is load-bearing. Two
        # renames cannot be one atomic act, so a crash between them must land
        # on the safe side: this way a reader sees a full count and no marker
        # and stays on the subscription, and the next 429 completes the
        # engagement. The reverse order would publish an engagement backed by
        # an unwritten count -- a false engagement, which is the very bug class
        # this change exists to close (AGORA #5034).
        _write_json(_counter_path(), info)
        _write_marker({
            "engaged_at": now.isoformat(timespec="seconds"),
            "by": "grip-auth-failover --note-429 (auto)",
            "bucket": "api-key",
            "entry": API_KEY_ENTRY,
            "reason": msg or f"auto: {count} consecutive transient 429s",
        })
        print(f"grip-auth-failover: AUTO-ENGAGED after {count} consecutive "
              "transient 429s — future spawns use the API-key bucket. "
              "Revert with `grip-auth-failover --reset`.")
    else:
        _write_json(_counter_path(), info)
        print(_note_429_line(count, already_engaged))
    return 0


def _note_429_line(count: int, already_engaged: bool) -> str:
    """What --note-429 prints when it records a 429 without engaging."""
    if already_engaged:
        return f"grip-auth-failover: already engaged; 429 recorded (total this window: {count})."
    recorded = f"grip-auth-failover: transient 429 recorded ({count}/{NOTE_429_THRESHOLD}); "
    if not _paid_allowed():
        return f"{recorded}not engaged -- {_PAID_REFUSED}"
    return f"{recorded}{NOTE_429_THRESHOLD - count} more within the window will auto-engage."


def _paid_gate(policy: str, source: str) -> tuple[str, str]:
    """Policy "engage" spends on the paid key, so it holds only with the opt-in."""
    if policy == "engage" and not _paid_allowed():
        return "notify", f"{source} asks for engage; refused without {PAID_OPT_IN_ENV}=1"
    return policy, source


def _cap_policy() -> tuple[str, str]:
    """What an account-wide usage cap does, and where that was set (GRIP#7274).

    $GRIP_AUTH_FAILOVER_ON_CAP wins, then "on_cap" in config/auth-failover.local.json
    under the GRIP directory (gitignored: it is a choice about this machine's spending),
    then the default, "notify". A value that is neither "notify" nor "engage" is ignored,
    so a typo is never read as consent to spend. "engage" also needs
    $GRIP_CLAUDE_ALLOW_PAID=1, or it is read as "notify" (_paid_gate)."""
    chosen = os.environ.get(CAP_POLICY_ENV, "").strip().lower()
    if chosen in CAP_POLICIES:
        return _paid_gate(chosen, CAP_POLICY_ENV)
    config = _read_json(get_claude_dir() / CAP_POLICY_FILE)
    chosen = str(config.get("on_cap", "")).strip().lower()
    if chosen in CAP_POLICIES:
        return _paid_gate(chosen, str(CAP_POLICY_FILE))
    return "notify", "default"


def _reset_from(arg: str) -> str:
    """The reset time a --note-cap argument carries.

    The REPL hook passes the harness's whole error text ("You've hit your session limit
    · resets 7:20am (UTC)"); a person or the relay passes the time alone, or nothing. A
    cap message gives the time it names, or "" when it names none, so a notice never
    reads "capped until Your credit balance is too low". Anything else is taken to be
    the time itself. Imported lazily, as in classify(); if the classifier cannot load,
    the argument is used as given, which is what --note-cap always did."""
    try:
        from rate_limit_classifier import cap_reset, classify as classify_message
    except Exception:
        return arg
    return cap_reset(arg) or ("" if classify_message(arg).is_cap else arg)


def _cap_notice_due(record: dict, reset: str, now: _dt.datetime) -> bool:
    """Whether a usage cap needs a notice: one per cap, not one per message (GRIP#7274).

    Due when the record holds no notice for this cap, when this cap names a different
    reset time, or when the last notice is older than NOTE_OK_COOLDOWN_SECS. A cap
    that names no reset time therefore repeats its notice at that interval. A notice
    stamped in the future (the clock moved back) counts as stale, never as fresh."""
    sent = _parse_stamp(record.get("notified_at"))
    if sent is None or record.get("cap_reset", "") != reset:
        return True
    age = (now - sent).total_seconds()
    return age < 0 or age > NOTE_OK_COOLDOWN_SECS


def _send_cap_notice(record: dict, until: str, now: _dt.datetime) -> None:
    """Push the cap notice. The record is marked sent only if the push landed; a
    failed push clears the mark, so the next message for this cap tries again."""
    if _notify("GRIP: subscription capped, waiting for the reset",
               f"The subscription hit its usage cap and is capped {until}. GRIP did not "
               f"switch to the paid API key and is waiting for the reset. {_OPT_IN}"):
        record["notified_at"] = now.isoformat(timespec="seconds")
    else:
        record.pop("notified_at", None)


def _wait_for_reset(reset: str) -> int:
    """Cap policy "notify": record the cap, say when it resets, engage nothing (GRIP#7274).

    The record goes beside MARKER (_cap_path), never into it: creating MARKER is what
    makes ~/.zshrc export the paid key. Every session that hits a cap writes its own
    error record, so one cap can arrive as dozens of messages, and a burst of identical
    notices teaches the operator to ignore them. So the notice goes out once per cap
    (_cap_notice_due), while this tool's one line is printed for every message."""
    now = _dt.datetime.now()
    record = _read_json(_cap_path())
    due = _cap_notice_due(record, reset, now)
    record.update(last_cap=now.isoformat(timespec="seconds"), cap_reset=reset)
    until = f"until {reset}" if reset else "until its reset"
    line = (f"grip-auth-failover: usage cap -- the subscription is capped {until}; the "
            "paid API key was not engaged (cap policy: notify).")
    if due:
        _send_cap_notice(record, until, now)
        print(f"{line} {_OPT_IN}")
    else:
        print(f"{line} The notice already went out at {record['notified_at']}; "
              "not sent again.")
    try:
        _write_json(_cap_path(), record)
    except OSError:
        pass  # a lost record can only repeat a notice, never hide one
    return 0


@_marker_locked
def note_cap(reset: str = "") -> int:
    """Record a USAGE CAP (session/weekly/monthly limit, quota, or 'credit balance
    too low') — the complement of --note-429 and the fix for silent-stop-on-cap. A
    cap is NOT a transient 429: it cannot be spun on (rate-limit-never-blocks.md).
    Every branch surfaces the cap to the operator. ``reset`` is the reset time or
    the harness's whole error text (_reset_from).

      NOT yet failed over, cap policy "notify" (the default, GRIP#7274)
          -> record the cap, notify with the reset time and wait. Nothing engages.
      NOT yet failed over, cap policy "engage", API key present
          -> ENGAGE the API-key bucket (a separate pool) + notify.
      already on the API-key bucket (the API key ITSELF is now capped /
      credit-empty) under either policy, OR policy "engage" with no API key
          -> notify "no usable fallback -> pausing to reset" and do NOT engage
             (nothing left to fail over to). The caller backs off to the reset;
             never a silent block.
    """
    reset = _reset_from(reset)
    info = _read_marker()
    already = info.get("bucket") == "api-key"
    if not already and _cap_policy()[0] == "notify":
        return _wait_for_reset(reset)
    api_present = _keychain_present(API_KEY_ENTRY)
    now = _dt.datetime.now().isoformat(timespec="seconds")
    reset_note = f" Resets at {reset}." if reset else ""

    if not already and api_present:
        info.update({
            "engaged_at": now,
            "by": "grip-auth-failover --note-cap (auto)",
            "bucket": "api-key",
            "entry": API_KEY_ENTRY,
            "reason": "usage-cap on subscription -> failed over to API-key bucket",
            "last_cap": now,
            "cap_reset": reset,
        })
        _write_marker(info)
        _notify("GRIP: subscription usage-cap -> failed over",
                "The subscription hit its usage cap. GRIP failed over to the "
                "API-key bucket (a separate rate-limit pool). If that also caps "
                "or is credit-empty, GRIP will surface it too -- never a silent stop.")
        print("grip-auth-failover: usage-cap on subscription -> ENGAGED the "
              "API-key bucket (separate pool) + notified. Revert with --reset.")
        return 0

    # Nothing left to fail over to: both buckets exhausted, or no API key.
    info["last_cap"] = now
    if reset:
        info["cap_reset"] = reset
    _write_marker(info)
    if already:
        detail = "Both the subscription AND the API-key bucket are usage-capped."
    else:
        detail = "The subscription is usage-capped and no API-key fallback is present."
    _notify("GRIP: usage-cap, no fallback left",
            f"{detail}{reset_note} GRIP is pausing to the reset -- NOT silently "
            "stopping. Top up grip-anthropic-external or wait for the reset.")
    print(f"grip-auth-failover: usage-cap, NO usable fallback. {detail}"
          f"{reset_note} Surfaced to operator; pause-to-reset.")
    return 0


def _model_named(msg: str) -> str:
    """The model a model-cap message names, or "". Imported lazily, as in classify();
    if the classifier cannot load, the notice still goes out, just unnamed."""
    try:
        from rate_limit_classifier import model_cap_model
    except Exception:
        return ""
    return model_cap_model(msg)


def note_model_cap(msg: str) -> int:
    """Record that ONE model's allowance is used up: notify, never engage (GRIP#7274).

    Every other model still runs on the subscription, so moving every new session
    onto the paid API key would be wrong. This deliberately reads and writes neither
    the marker nor the 429 counter, which is also why it takes no marker lock.
    """
    who = _model_named(msg) or "One model"
    _notify("GRIP: one model's allowance is used up",
            f"{who} has used up its allowance. Other models still run on the "
            "subscription, so GRIP did not switch to the paid API key. Run /model "
            "to switch model.")
    print(f"grip-auth-failover: {who}'s allowance is used up; the API-key bucket was "
          "not engaged (other models still run on the subscription). Run /model to "
          "switch model.")
    return 0


def _parse_stamp(stamp) -> _dt.datetime | None:
    """A marker or counter timestamp (local, naive ISO-8601), or None."""
    try:
        return _dt.datetime.fromisoformat(stamp) if stamp else None
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return None


def _last_throttle(marker: dict, counter: dict) -> _dt.datetime | None:
    """The most recent sign of throttling: the engagement, the last 429 or the last cap."""
    stamps = (_parse_stamp(marker.get("engaged_at")), _parse_stamp(marker.get("last_cap")),
              _parse_stamp(counter.get("last_429")))
    known = [s for s in stamps if s is not None]
    return max(known) if known else None


@_marker_locked
def note_ok(msg: str) -> int:
    """Record that the SUBSCRIPTION served a call, and end an automatic engagement
    once the throttle is safely over (GRIP#7274).

    Three cases leave the marker alone: nothing is engaged; the engagement was made
    by hand, which is the operator's choice and ends only with --reset; or the last
    throttle is newer than NOTE_OK_COOLDOWN_SECS. Otherwise the marker and the 429
    window go, as with --reset, and the recovery is announced as the engagement was.
    """
    marker = _read_marker()
    if marker.get("bucket") != "api-key":
        print("grip-auth-failover: --note-ok: not engaged; nothing to clear.")
        return 0
    if not str(marker.get("by", "")).endswith(AUTO_ENGAGED_SUFFIX):
        print("grip-auth-failover: --note-ok: engaged by hand; only --reset ends it.")
        return 0
    last = _last_throttle(marker, _read_counter())
    age = (_dt.datetime.now() - last).total_seconds() if last else 0.0
    if age < NOTE_OK_COOLDOWN_SECS:
        print(f"grip-auth-failover: --note-ok: last throttle {int(age // 60)} min ago; "
              f"keeping the API-key bucket until {NOTE_OK_COOLDOWN_SECS // 60} min have passed.")
        return 0
    # Marker first: a crash after it leaves only a stale 429 window, which engages nothing.
    MARKER.unlink(missing_ok=True)
    _counter_path().unlink(missing_ok=True)
    mins = int(age // 60)
    print(f"grip-auth-failover: AUTO-RECOVERED — the subscription served a call {mins} min "
          "after the last throttle; marker removed, future spawns use the subscription again.")
    _notify("GRIP auth failover recovered",
            f"The subscription is serving again ({msg or 'a subscription call succeeded'}), "
            f"{mins} min after the last throttle. New claude spawns and the relay are back "
            "on the subscription.")
    return 0


def status() -> int:
    # Read the counter FIRST: that call performs the legacy migration, and
    # reporting before healing meant the first --status on an affected host
    # printed exactly the false "subscription" line that hid the incident for
    # five days, then repaired the state after the output had gone out
    # (AGORA #5034).
    window = _read_counter()
    bucket = _active_bucket()
    info = _read_marker()
    if info.get("bucket") == "api-key":
        print(f"grip-auth-failover: ENGAGED since {info.get('engaged_at')} "
              f"by {info.get('by')} — reason: {info.get('reason')}")
    else:
        print("grip-auth-failover: marker OFF (subscription-preferring)")
    count = window.get("consecutive_429s", 0)
    if count:
        print(f"  consecutive transient 429s this window: {count}/"
              f"{NOTE_429_THRESHOLD} (window started {window.get('window_start')})")
    if count >= NOTE_429_THRESHOLD and info.get("bucket") != "api-key":
        # Armed but not engaged: the marker write failed or the process died
        # between the two renames. The next SINGLE 429 completes the
        # engagement, which is exactly the event this tool promises cannot
        # happen -- so say so rather than leaving the host one hit away from a
        # silent bucket switch. Never auto-complete the marker here: that would
        # engage without a 429 (AGORA #5034 round 2).
        print("  WARNING: the counter is at or past the threshold with no "
              "engagement recorded — the NEXT transient 429 will auto-engage. "
              "Clear it with `grip-auth-failover --reset`.")
    print(f"  active bucket for future spawns: {bucket}")
    policy, source = _cap_policy()
    print(f"  usage-cap policy: {policy} ({source})")
    print(f"  API-key bucket: "
          f"{'PRESENT' if _keychain_present(API_KEY_ENTRY) else 'ABSENT'}")
    print(f"  subscription credential: "
          f"{'PRESENT' if _oauth_present() else 'ABSENT'}")
    return 0


def classify(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    """Print whether a 429-ish message is transient (retry) or a usage-cap (wait).

    Delegates to lib/rate_limit_classifier — the single mechanical discriminator
    for rules/rate-limit-never-blocks.md, so the "distinguish at the call site"
    prose above is now one tested call, not per-site eyeballing. The import is
    lazy: only this command, --note-model-cap and --note-cap pay for it;
    --engage/--note-429/--reset/--status are untouched. Exit code encodes the verdict for
    shell callers:
      0 = transient / retryable (retry then failover)
      4 = usage-cap (bounded wait to reset, or engage the API-key bucket)
      5 = one model's allowance used up (switch model; never engage the API-key bucket)
      2 = no message given
      3 = classifier module not importable (honest failure, never a silent guess).
    """
    msg = " ".join(argv).strip()
    if not msg:
        print("grip-auth-failover: --classify needs a message to classify.")
        return 2
    _lib = str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "lib")
    if _lib not in sys.path:
        sys.path.insert(0, _lib)
    try:
        from rate_limit_classifier import classify as _classify_msg
    except Exception as exc:  # honest failure — do not pretend to have classified
        print(f"grip-auth-failover: --classify unavailable — the classifier "
              f"module is not importable ({exc}).")
        return 3
    verdict = _classify_msg(msg)
    action, code = _classify_action(verdict)
    print(f"class:     {verdict.kind}")
    print(f"retryable: {'yes' if verdict.retryable else 'no'} -> {action}")
    print(f"reason:    {verdict.reason}")
    return code


def _classify_action(verdict) -> tuple[str, int]:
    """The advice and exit code --classify gives for a verdict (see classify())."""
    if verdict.is_model_cap:
        return "switch model with /model; do not engage the API-key bucket", 5
    if verdict.retryable:
        return "retry / failover (never report 'blocked')", 0
    return "bounded wait to reset, or engage the API-key bucket", 4


_DISPATCH = {
    "--engage": lambda argv: engage(" ".join(argv)),
    "--note-429": lambda argv: note_429(" ".join(argv)),
    "--note-cap": lambda argv: note_cap(" ".join(argv)),
    "--note-model-cap": lambda argv: note_model_cap(" ".join(argv)),
    "--note-ok": lambda argv: note_ok(" ".join(argv)),
    "--classify": classify,
    "--reset": lambda argv: reset(),
    "--status": lambda argv: status(),
    "--help": lambda argv: (print(__doc__) or 0),
    "-h": lambda argv: (print(__doc__) or 0),
}


def main(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    cmd = argv[0] if argv else "--status"
    handler = _DISPATCH.get(cmd)
    if handler is None:
        print(__doc__)
        return 2
    return handler(argv[1:])


if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
